Volume = {5},
Month = {DEC 14},
Abstract = {The endolymphatic sac (ES) is a cystic organ that is a part of the inner
   ear and is connected to the cochlea and vestibule. The ES is thought to
   be involved in inner ear ion homeostasis and fluid volume regulation for
   the maintenance of hearing and balance function. Many ion channels,
   transporters, and exchangers have been identified in the ES luminal
   epithelium, mainly in animal studies, but there has been no functional
   study investigating ion transport using human ES tissue. We designed the
   first functional experiments on electrogenic transport in human ES and
   investigated the contribution of K+ channels in the electrogenic
   transport, which has been rarely identified, even in animal studies,
   using electrophysiological/pharmacological and molecular biological
   methods. As a result, we identified functional and molecular evidence
   for the essential participation of K+ channels in the electrogenic
   transport of human ES epithelium. The identified K+ channels involved in
   the electrogenic transport were KCNN2, KCNJ14, KCNK2, and KCNK6, and the
   K+ transports via those channels are thought to play an important role
   in the maintenance of the unique ionic milieu of the inner ear fluid.},
